  "articleBody": "When storm runoff or roof leaks penetrate your living space, repairing the sheetrock involves much more than hanging fresh boards. Here is a guide to handling storm-damaged sheetrock before hiring a crew.\n\nOverview: Water behind wall cavities can saturate insulation, framing, and drywall paper. A sound repair restores both structural integrity and clean indoor air.\n\nKey Benefits: A planned replacement removes hidden moisture, stops future fungal growth, and produces flat, properly textured surfaces that blend invisibly.\n\nProcess Breakdown:\n- Stop the leak source and eliminate standing water immediately.\n- Measure moisture using proper meters against unaffected control walls instead of relying on touch.\n- Strip unsalvageable, saturated drywall and insulation.\n- Set up dehumidifiers and air movers until framing reaches dry standards.\n- Rebuild framing or blocking, hang new panels, tape, apply texture, prime, and repaint.\n\nReal Examples: Homeowners frequently try to save sheetrock that feels dry on front, only to find damp insulation behind it weeks later. Proper containment and cavity evaluation prevent repeat repairs.\n\nWhat to Do Next: Abraham Becerra and the crew at HH Remodeling LLC handle storm damage restoration, drywall installation, framing, and finish painting across Denver and Aurora. Call 303-505-0866 to inspect your storm-damaged walls.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
